      <h1>What to Do When You Inherit a House You Don't Want in Texas</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Not every inherited home is a gift you're ready to keep. If you're out of state, out of time, or simply not interested, you have more options than you might think.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>Inheriting a house sounds like good news, and often it is. But for a lot of families it arrives at a hard time and with strings attached: a property in a city you don't live in, a home full of a loved one's belongings, repairs that were put off for years, and a monthly bill for taxes and insurance that starts immediately. If you're sitting on an inherited house you don't want, you are far from alone, and you are not stuck.</p>
          <p>We're licensed Texas Realtors, not attorneys or tax advisors. This guide covers the real estate side of the decision. For legal or tax questions specific to your situation, consult a probate attorney or CPA.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="first-step">First, Give Yourself a Moment to Get the Facts</h2>
        <p>There's usually no need to decide on day one. Before choosing a direction, it helps to know a few things: who legally owns the property right now, whether there is a mortgage or other lien on it, what the home is worth, and whether probate is open or still needs to be started. Those answers shape which options are available to you.</p>
        <p>What you don't want to do is let the house sit with no plan. Vacant homes cost money every month, and that cost doesn't pause while the family decides.</p>

        <h2 id="options">Your Options</h2>

        <div class="qa-list">
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Sell it on the open market</h4>
            <p>A traditional listing often brings the highest price, especially if the home is in decent condition. The tradeoff is time and effort: preparing the home, showings, negotiations, and a closing timeline that depends on the buyer's financing. If you're out of state, an agent who understands inherited and probate property can handle most of it for you.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Sell it for cash, as-is</h4>
            <p>If the home needs work, is full of belongings, or you simply want it done, a direct cash sale can close quickly with no repairs, no cleaning out, and no showings. The price is typically lower than a fully prepared market listing, so it's worth comparing both with real numbers before deciding.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Buy out or sell to a co-heir</h4>
            <p>If several family members inherited the home together, one person may want to keep it. A buyout lets that person take full ownership while everyone else receives their share in cash. A clear market valuation makes this much easier to settle fairly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Rent it out</h4>
            <p>Some heirs keep the home as a rental for a while. This can work if the property is in good shape and you're comfortable being a landlord, or paying a manager. It's a real commitment, though, and not a fit for most people who inherited a house they don't want.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Disclaim the inheritance</h4>
            <p>In some situations, a beneficiary can formally refuse an inheritance, which is called disclaiming. The property then passes as if you had not been a beneficiary. There are strict rules, including timing and the requirement that you haven't already accepted benefits from the property, and it affects who receives the home next. This is a legal decision, so talk to a probate attorney before taking any step that could affect your ability to disclaim.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="carrying-costs">What Holding It Really Costs</h2>
        <p>Even a paid-off house is not free to hold. Property taxes, homeowners insurance, utilities, lawn care, and basic maintenance all continue. If there's a mortgage, those payments continue too. Vacant homes can also run into insurance problems, since many standard policies limit coverage when a house sits empty for an extended period. It's worth calling the insurance company early and being honest about the situation.</p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong>A quick way to think about it:</strong> Add up the monthly carrying costs and multiply by the number of months it would take to sell. That number is the real price of waiting, and it often makes a faster, simpler path look a lot more reasonable.
        </div>

        <h2 id="taxes">What About Taxes?</h2>
        <p>The tax picture is often better than families expect. Heirs generally receive a stepped-up basis, which means your starting value for tax purposes is the home's value at the date of death rather than what the original owner paid. Selling soon after inheriting often results in little or no capital gain. We cover this in more detail in our guide on <a href="/blog/how-capital-gains-tax-works-on-inherited-property-in-texas">how capital gains tax works on inherited property in Texas</a>. A CPA can confirm how it applies to you.</p>

        <h2 id="steps">A Simple Order of Operations</h2>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 1</div>
            <h4>Confirm ownership and probate status</h4>
            <p>Find out whether probate has been opened, who the executor or administrator is, and whether you currently have authority to act. A probate attorney can answer this quickly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 2</div>
            <h4>Secure the property and keep it insured</h4>
            <p>Change the locks if needed, keep utilities on at a basic level, and make sure insurance is in force and appropriate for a vacant home.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 3</div>
            <h4>Get an honest valuation</h4>
            <p>Knowing what the home is worth, as-is and after repairs, tells you which options make financial sense. This is also the number you'll want for tax purposes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 4</div>
            <h4>Talk with the other heirs</h4>
            <p>If you aren't the only beneficiary, align early. Surprises between family members cause more delays than almost anything else.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 5</div>
            <h4>Pick a path and move</h4>
            <p>Once you have the facts, choose the option that fits your timeline and goals, then put a plan and a date on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

